well I think for me the importance of  the Himalayas is is the extent to which  it dominates that part of Asia really it  controls the climate it's a major area  of cultural and biological diversity and  it's also of course the coming together  of many interesting and fascinating  countries in that region and what  happens in the Himalayas has an impact  on the whole of that part of Asia and  particularly of course on China and  India in particular but also Southeast  Asia so the Himalayas is a kind of a  pivotal place I think for for an  academic institution that's that's  interested in the in the future of that  part of Asia and those two great  economies in particular the Himalayas is  right at the center of all of that the  Yale School of Forestry environmental  studies has had a really strong and deep  and ongoing relationship with countries  in the Himalayas not only through our  ongoing work with colleagues in Bhutan  but also through long established  programs in the poor particularly  professor birch and the work that he did  there but also with colleagues in India  and in indeed the the long relationship  that the school has had with forestry in  the Government of India based out of  dehradun and our alums who are  associated in that part of India so so  in a way this is a very natural part of  the world for the school of Forestry and  environmental studies to be engaged with  and because it plays to our long  historical strengths it plays to a real  concentration of our alumni and what I  like in particular this is that it  crosses borders it's not a relationship  with a particular country its  relationship with a region and I think  that's really very very important and of  course that historical relationship with  with the Himalayas  also plays out in the present day I mean  we have students right now from India  students from Bhutan students from from  the poor and they're an active and  vibrant part of our of our Himalayan  program and what's wonderful is that  they'll go back to the region and  they'll join our existing alumni body in  that region and they are helped to make  things happen on the ground and the  school is very much concerned with what  happens on the ground what happens in  practice our PhD students from the  region are masters students from the  region even interacting with  undergraduates here Yale who are from or  have an interest in the region so  there's a lot of concentration on the  Himalayas here which i think is a really  really great thing I think from from the  opportunities that I've had to visit the  Himalayas what's been underlined to me  is the kind of the complexity of the  region the kind of cultural complexity  but also the huge challenges they face  in terms of infrastructure but also the  rapid changes that are going on in that  region just the same as they're going on  it everywhere else around the world it's  wonderful for me to see our alumni  engaged in different parts of these  processes it was fantastic for me to  have my eyes opened to some of that some  of these issues many many different  kinds of issues biological ones physical  ones cultural ones and I think that that  really the Himalayas is a wonderful  place where we really can bring these  three ideas of environment livelihoods  and culture and bring them together to  really understand what's happening in  the region  you
 

